{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3978","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md@sha256:89649c534aff11192bb29e2e26319ddba64cd605e30813f775e5f193417e7bfd"},"owner_reference":"3978","content":"# Architecture Spec: Primary-Quota 403 Classified Retryable (#3978)\n\n**Scope**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` (primary edit site),\n`gh_client.py` (import + two call-site edits: `_graphql_request`'s delay fallback, and\n`probe_backend_status`'s classification reuse \u2014 \u00a78), `github_client.py` (`make_github_client`'s\nretry configuration \u2014 \u00a79), `parsing.py` (`view_result_from_local_item`'s missing `number` field \u2014\n\u00a710), `models.py` (deletion of dead `ViewItemResultCompact` \u2014 \u00a711), `sync_engine.py`/`server.py`/\n`backends/github_backend.py` (verify-only, no interface change).\nExtends the existing module design in\n[`backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../../../plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md)\n\u00a7 \"Error Handling Pattern\" and \u00a7 \"Module: gh_client.py\" \u2014 this document does not redefine that\nmodule map, it adds the classification/delay/retry-bound signals inside it plus one unrelated,\nowner-added field-population fix in `parsing.py`.\n\n**2026-09-28 scope amendment**: the repo owner reviewed this document's original \u00a72 Q6 decision\n(`probe_backend_status` out of scope) and \u00a73.4's \"file a separate backlog item\" recommendation\n(bounding PyGithub's in-transport retry), plus one unrelated defect observed live on this item\nitself (`backlog_view`/`backlog view` returning `\"number\": null`), and added all three to this\nitem's scope: \"add them to scope. looks aligned to me.\" Every decision below this line that predates\nthe amendment is kept as originally written; each place the amendment supersedes or extends a\ndecision is called out explicitly inline, and the three new designs are \u00a78 (A), \u00a79 (B), \u00a710 (C). A\nfourth, later addition \u2014 deleting dead `ViewItemResultCompact` (\u00a711) \u2014 is **not** part of this\n2026-09-28 owner amendment; it comes from the SAM plan's own auto-mode planning decision D4, made\nduring task decomposition, and supersedes only this document's original \u00a77 Non-Goal on that class.\n\nEffort matches the item's own estimate: one classification branch, one shared delay-conversion\nhelper, one `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` entry (justified below), no new Pydantic models, no\nnew exception classes, no schema change. The three amendment items are each similarly small: one\nreused-classifier branch (A), one retry subclass plus one constructor argument (B), and one\nconstructor-argument fix to an already-declared, never-populated Pydantic field (C). D4's addition\n(\u00a711) is smaller still: one class deletion, gated on a reference search, no code path change.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n`sync_state._classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch treats a primary-quota refusal\n(`RateLimitExceededException`, GitHub's \"API rate limit exceeded\u2026\" message, no `Retry-After`) as\n`NON_RETRYABLE` because it only recognizes `Retry-After` or a \"secondary rate limit\" message\nsubstring. The fix adds a third signal \u2014 primary-limit detection mirroring the codebase's own\nexisting `gh_client._is_rate_limited` precedent \u2014 ahead of the same 403 return statement, and adds\none shared helper that converts `x-ratelimit-reset` (epoch seconds) into a bounded delay when\n`Retry-After` is absent. That helper is called from both the REST classifier's\n`retry_after_seconds` and the GraphQL `GitHubRateLimitedError` construction in `gh_client.py`\n(Acceptance Criterion 9), satisfying the orchestrator's \"fix once where all callers route through\"\nconstraint. `sync_state` gains no new import of `gh_client`; the existing one-way dependency\n(`gh_client` imports `sync_state`) is preserved.\n\nTracing the actual PyGithub call path (Q2, \u00a73) surfaced one additional, in-scope, evidence-backed\ngap: PyGithub's own default retry transport can exhaust its budget and raise\n`requests.exceptions.RetryError` \u2014 an `OSError` subclass that is *not* a `GithubException` \u2014 which\ntoday falls into `classify_sync_error`'s generic `OSError` branch and comes out `NON_RETRYABLE`.\nThat defeats this fix's intent on the one path where PyGithub's own retry-until-reset behavior\ngenuinely fails. \u00a73.3 covers why this is in scope despite not being named in the item text.\n\nThe 2026-09-28 amendment adds three more pieces, all evidence-gathered and designed below: **(A)**\n`probe_backend_status` (`gh_client.py` ~2047-2048) is rewired to reuse `sync_state`'s own\n`classify_github_failure` \u2014 the exact classifier this document already builds \u2014 instead of its\nindependent, over-broad \"every 403 is a rate limit\" rule (\u00a78). **(B)** `make_github_client`\n(`github_client.py` ~912) gains a small `GithubRetry` subclass that bounds how long a single REST\ncall can block inside PyGithub's transport on a primary-limit 403, without touching the retry\ncounts or backoff PyGithub already applies correctly to secondary limits and 5xx responses (\u00a79).\n**(C)** `backlog_view` (MCP) and `backlog view` (CLI) reporting `\"number\": null` for an item that\ndemonstrably has a GitHub issue number (#3978 itself) is root-caused to a declared-but-never-\npopulated Pydantic field and fixed at its single producer (\u00a710) \u2014 this defect is unrelated to the\nrate-limit fix in every other section of this document; it rides in the same item only because the\nowner explicitly said to bundle it here.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design Decisions (Q1\u2013Q6)\n\nEach open question from the feature-context artifact gets a decision here. All six are resolved \u2014\nnone deferred.\n\n### Q1 \u2014 Bound and margin for a reset-derived delay\n\n**Decision**: Convert `x-ratelimit-reset` (epoch seconds) to `(reset - now) + 1.0` seconds (the\n`+1s` margin matches PyGithub's own `GithubRetry` precedent, \u00a73.2), clamped to\n`[0.0, _MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS]` where the ceiling is **3900 seconds** (65 minutes \u2014\nGitHub's documented primary window is 60 minutes; the extra 5 minutes tolerates ordinary clock\nskew without accepting a corrupted timestamp, e.g. a value that is off by orders of magnitude).\nPer plan decision D1 (SAM plan `P999000ed` context), the helper returns `None` when the computed `(reset - now) + 1.0` value is `<= 0` \u2014 concretely, a reset exactly `now` still yields `1.0` (not `None`), and a reset one second or more in the past yields a computed value `<= 0`, so `None`. The helper also returns `None` when the header is absent or non-numeric. It does **not** fabricate a fallback value. `retry_after_seconds`'s existing contract already means\n\"no hint\" in that case, and `sync_engine._attempt_sync`'s existing\n`max(_compute_backoff_delay(attempt), state.retry_after or 0.0)` already supplies the community-\nstandard \"wait at least a fixed floor\" fallback (30s / 120s schedule) without this change needing\nto invent a second fallback mechanism. No change to `sync_engine.py`'s `MAX_RETRIES` or\n`_BACKOFF_DELAYS` \u2014 the existing `max()` combination already lets a real GitHub-supplied delay\ndominate the fixed schedule, which is exactly what Scenario 2 (feature-context) needs.\n\n**Why not a smaller cap or no cap**: a hard 3600s cap sized exactly to the window would reject a\nvalue at 3599.5s after normal skew; no cap at all reopens Gap 1 (an absurd value sleeping the\nevent loop for years on a corrupted header) that AC2 explicitly rules out.\n\n**This bound governs the value *reported to callers*** (`retry_after` on an MCP response, the sync\nloop's backoff floor). \u00a79 (B) adds a separate, much smaller bound on how long PyGithub's transport\nmay itself sleep *inside one REST call* before that call returns control to its caller at all \u2014 the\ntwo bounds serve different purposes and now compose: \u00a79 makes the primary-limit case surface\nalmost immediately as an exception; this Q1 bound then governs how large a wait hint that\nexception is allowed to report.\n\n### Q2 \u2014 What actually reaches the classifier, given PyGithub's in-transport retry\n\n**Decision**: Documented in full in \u00a73; the classifier fix is confirmed necessary and reachable,\nand one additional in-scope change (`RetryError` in `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`, \u00a73.3) is\nadded because tracing showed it directly determines whether this fix is observable on REST call\npaths. The in-transport hang itself (GithubRetry sleeping synchronously inside a single PyGithub\ncall, up to ~65 minutes) is upstream PyGithub behavior, not something `_classify_github_exception`\ncan change, and was **not** part of this fix's original scope \u2014 see \u00a73.4 for the original\nreasoning. **Superseded 2026-09-28**: the owner added exactly this gap to scope; \u00a79 designs the\nbound. \u00a73.4's \"recommend filing a separate backlog item\" is withdrawn for this specific gap \u2014\nit is being done here instead.\n\n### Q3 \u2014 Which signal identifies a primary-limit 403\n\n**Decision**: A new `_is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)` predicate, checked inside the\nexisting 403 branch alongside (not replacing) the current `has_retry_after` /\n`_SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER` check. It recognizes the signal from whichever of three places\nPyGithub happened to preserve it (\u00a73.2 explains why more than one is needed):\n\n1. `isinstance(exc, RateLimitExceededException)` \u2014 true whenever PyGithub's own\n   `Requester.createException` recognized *either* a primary or secondary rate-limit message; safe\n   to treat as retryable unconditionally because `createException` only assigns this type when\n   `isRateLimitError()` was already true.\n2. The casefolded message starts with `\"api rate limit exceeded\"` \u2014 GitHub's documented primary-\n   quota wording, duplicated locally (mirrors the existing `_SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER` /\n   `Requester.isPrimaryRateLimitError` precedent) because `sync_state` must not import `gh_client`.\n3. `x-ratelimit-remaining == \"0\"` on the 403 response \u2014 the fallback for the one case where (1)\n   and (2) are both unavailable: PyGithub's `GithubRetry.increment()` raising a **plain**\n   `GithubException(status, content, headers)` when the response body fails JSON parsing (\u00a73.2's\n   \"malformed body\" branch). Headers still pass through intact in that branch even though the\n   message does not, which is exactly why a header-only fallback is needed and why `_is_rate_limited`\n   in `gh_client.py` already carries the identical fallback for the GraphQL path.\n\nVerified non-regression: `GithubException(403, \"Forbidden\", {})` (AC5's fixture) has no headers,\nso signal 3 evaluates `str(None) == \"0\"` \u2192 `False`; signals 1 and 2 are also false; the branch\nstill returns `NON_RETRYABLE`.\n\nThis predicate is deliberately implemented once, in `sync_state.py`, as the codebase's single\ndefinition of \"a 403 is a primary-quota rate limit\" for classification purposes. \u00a78 (A) reuses the\n*function that calls it* (`classify_github_failure`) rather than re-deriving the signal a fourth\ntime in `gh_client.py` \u2014 see \u00a78 for why that is the correct reuse boundary given the import\ndirection.\n\n### Q4 \u2014 Does the reset-derived delay also cover 429?\n\n**Decision**: Yes. `retry_after_seconds` already scopes its `Retry-After` lookup to\n`{_HTTP_FORBIDDEN, _HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS}` (403 and 429) \u2014 the new `x-ratelimit-reset` fallback\nuses the identical status set, so a primary-limit refusal gets the same wait hint whichever status\nGitHub chose to send it on, consistent with GitHub's own docs (\"you will receive a 403 or 429\nresponse\") and with `gh_client._is_rate_limited`'s existing 403/429 (plus 400 for GraphQL) scope.\n\n### Q5 \u2014 Header precedence when both are present\n\n**Decision**: `Retry-After` wins, matching the Change Frame and PyGithub's own `GithubRetry`\n(which only computes the primary-limit reset-based backoff in the branch where `Retry-After` is\n*absent* \u2014 see \u00a73.2). Implementation: the new reset-delay helper is only consulted when\n`parse_retry_after_header` already returned `None`; no new precedence logic is needed beyond that\nexisting `if`/fallback shape.\n\n### Q6 \u2014 Is `gh_client.probe_backend_status`'s any-403-is-rate-limited rule in scope?\n\n**Original decision (kept for record)**: Out of scope, per the item's own auto-mode default.\n`probe_backend_status` (line ~2047) answers a different question (\"is the backend currently\nrate-limited for status-reporting purposes\") through a deliberately independent, coarser check;\naligning it with the classifier's more precise signal is a defensible follow-up but is not named by\nthis item's Files or Acceptance Criteria, and touching it would add a fourth call site with its own\ntest surface to a change whose entire premise is \"one classification branch.\" Recommended filing a\nseparate backlog item.\n\n**Superseded 2026-09-28**: the repo owner reviewed this recommendation together with \u00a73.4's and the\n`backlog_view` `number: null` defect and added all three to scope: \"add them to scope. looks\naligned to me.\" Q6 is now **in scope** \u2014 \u00a78 designs it, reusing `classify_github_failure` (the\n`_classify_github_exception`-backed classifier this same document builds in \u00a74.1) instead of\nintroducing a fourth independent rate-limit signal.\n\n---\n\n## 3. PyGithub Call-Path Trace (Q2 detail)\n\nTraced against this repo's installed `PyGithub` (`.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/`,\nversion pinned by `uv.lock`) and `urllib3==2.7.0`, `requests==2.33.1` (installed versions).\n\n### 3.1 `make_github_client` uses PyGithub's default retry, not no retry\n\n`github_client.make_github_client()` (line 912) calls `Github(auth=..., base_url=..., timeout=...)`\nwith **no** `retry=` argument. `github.MainClass.Github.default_retry = GithubRetry()` (class\nattribute, `MainClass.py:169`) is the default value of the `retry` parameter\n(`MainClass.py:187`), so every client this factory builds carries a live `GithubRetry()` instance\nwith GitHub's own primary/secondary rate-limit awareness. `Requester.__init__` stores it as\n`self.retry`, and `HTTPSRequestsConnectionClass.__init__` (`Requester.py:182-187`) mounts it as\n`requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(max_retries=self.retry, ...)` on the session \u2014 confirmed by reading\nthat constructor directly, not inferred.\n\n**Confirmed 2026-09-28 (\u00a79 evidence gathering)**: `git show 6befa35e3` (#3968, \"stop GitHub requests\nhanging\") added exactly one deadline mechanism, and it does not reach this call path.\n`github_client.py`'s diff from that commit adds `graphql_total_timeout_seconds()` and\n`GRAPHQL_TOTAL_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT = 60`, consumed by `gh_client._call_with_deadline`, which wraps a\nGraphQL POST in a worker thread and `.join(timeout=graphql_total_timeout_seconds())`. That deadline\nis GraphQL-specific \u2014 REST calls made through PyGithub's object interface (`repo.get_issues()`,\n`issue.edit()`, every six-plus call site in `backends/github_backend.py`, `probe_backend_status`\nitself) go straight through the `Github`/`Requester`/`GithubRetry` stack traced in this section,\nwith no wrapping thread and no total-wall-clock deadline of any kind. `Github(timeout=...)`\n(`DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 30`, unchanged by #3968) bounds only the gap between two individual socket\nreads, not a request's total duration, and does not fire while `GithubRetry.increment()` is inside\na `time.sleep()` call between attempts (confirmed by reading `Requester.py`'s connection-class\nconstruction: the `timeout` value is passed to the underlying `urllib3`/`requests` HTTP call, not\nto the retry object). **No existing deadline bounds a REST call's in-library rate-limit wait.**\n\u00a79 designs the missing bound.\n\n### 3.2 What `GithubRetry.increment()` does with a 403 (`github/GithubRetry.py`)\n\n`GithubRetry.__init__` adds `403` to `status_forcelist` (already includes 500-599) and adds `GET`,\n`POST` to `allowed_methods` (`GithubRetry.py:84-85`). `urllib3`'s connection pool calls\n`retries.is_retry(method, response.status, has_retry_after)` before ever calling `increment()`\n(confirmed in `urllib3/connectionpool.py:943-950`); for a 403 on GET/POST this is `True`, so\n`GithubRetry.increment()` runs **inside the transport layer, before PyGithub's caller sees\nanything**:\n\n- **`Retry-After` header present** \u2192 logs and falls through to `super().increment(...)` (ordinary\n  urllib3 backoff/retry, no primary-limit-aware delay computed here).\n- **No `Retry-After`, response body is valid JSON with a `message`** \u2192 checks\n  `Requester.isRateLimitError(message)`.\n  - **True** (primary or secondary wording) \u2192 computes `retry.get_backoff_time()` for primary as\n    `(reset_epoch - now) + 1` seconds when `X-RateLimit-Reset` is present and parses as a digit\n    string, else `0.0`; for secondary, `secondary_rate_wait` (default 60s, `DEFAULT_SECONDARY_\n    RATE_WAIT`, `GithubRetry.py:51`). **No exception is raised at this point** \u2014 `GithubRetry.\n    increment()` returns a `Retry` object whose `get_backoff_time` is monkey-patched to this\n    computed value (`GithubRetry.py:176-184`). `urlopen()` sleeps that computed backoff\n    (potentially up to ~an hour for primary, synchronously, inside the caller's single PyGithub\n    call) and retries the request, up to `Retry.total`'s budget (urllib3 default **`total=10`**,\n    confirmed at `.venv/.../urllib3/util/retry.py:221`, unmodified by `GithubRetry.__init__`; the\n    separate `status` counter defaults to `None` at the same location, meaning 403/5xx retries are\n    governed purely by `total` unless a caller sets `status=` explicitly).\n  - **False** (body parses but doesn't look like a rate limit \u2014 e.g. plain \"Forbidden\") \u2192\n    `raise Requester.createException(response.status, response.headers, content)`, a **normal\n    `GithubException`** (here `RateLimitExceededException` is *not* constructed, since\n    `createException` itself re-checks `isRateLimitError` and got `False`). This propagates\n    straight out of `urlopen()`/`requests` to the caller, reaching `_classify_github_exception`\n    immediately, no sleep. This is the path AC5's plain-`Forbidden` fixture exercises and must\n    keep rejecting.\n- **No `Retry-After`, body fails JSON parsing / has no `message`** \u2192 the code falls into its own\n  exception-wrapping branch and raises a **plain** `GithubException(response.status, content,\n  response.headers)` directly (`GithubRetry.py:126-129`) \u2014 *not* through `createException`, so it\n  is never typed as `RateLimitExceededException` even when the underlying condition is a genuine\n  primary-limit refusal. Response headers (`x-ratelimit-remaining`, `x-ratelimit-reset`) are still\n  attached to this exception, since they come from `response.headers` unconditionally. This is\n  exactly the case Q3's header-based fallback signal (\u00a72, Q3 item 3) exists for.\n\n`Requester.createException` (`Requester.py:936-963`, confirmed by direct read) is a public\n`classmethod` taking `(status: int, headers: dict, output: dict)` (`output` already\n`json.loads`-parsed) and returning a correctly-typed `GithubException` \u2014 assigning\n`RateLimitExceededException` specifically when `status == 403 and cls.isRateLimitError(lc_message)`.\n\u00a79's bounded-retry subclass reuses this exact factory to raise the same exception type PyGithub\nwould eventually raise anyway, just without first sleeping through the primary-limit backoff.\n\n### 3.3 When `total` is exhausted: `requests.exceptions.RetryError`, not `GithubException`\n\nIf `GithubRetry`'s repeated sleep-and-retry cycle still returns 403 after all `total=10` attempts\n(possible if the account stays over quota across every retry, or clock skew keeps `X-RateLimit-\nReset` appearing not-yet-reached), `super().increment(...)` \u2014 called from inside `GithubRetry\n.increment()`'s own `try` block \u2014 raises `urllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError`. That block's own\n`except (MaxRetryError, GithubException): raise` re-raises it unchanged (confirmed,\n`GithubRetry.py:191-192`), so it propagates out of `urlopen()` as `MaxRetryError`.\n`requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter.send()` catches `MaxRetryError` and \u2014 because its `.reason` is a\n`urllib3.exceptions.ResponseError` for a status-forcelist exhaustion \u2014 re-raises it as\n`requests.exceptions.RetryError` (confirmed, `requests/adapters.py:662-668`). `RetryError` extends\n`RequestException(IOError)` (confirmed, `requests/exceptions.py:13,132`), i.e. it **is an\n`OSError` subclass and is *not* a `GithubException`.** It does **not** carry the failed response's\nheaders \u2014 `RetryError.__init__` (via `RequestException`) takes only a message and an optional\n`response`/`request`, and nothing in the `MaxRetryError \u2192 RetryError` conversion path attaches the\nlast `X-RateLimit-Reset` value anywhere `retry_after_seconds`'s `GithubException`-typed branch\n(\u00a74.1) could read it. A `retry_after` derived from this path is therefore always `None` \u2014 accepted\nby Q1's \"no fabrication\" decision, with `sync_engine`'s own fixed backoff floor as the fallback.\n\n`sync_state.classify_sync_error` checks `isinstance(exc, GithubException)` before its generic\n`isinstance(exc, (OSError, ValueError))` fallback (`sync_state.py:562-576`). A `RetryError` skips\nthe `GithubException` branch entirely (this fix's new signal never runs on it) and lands in the\n`OSError` branch, which returns `NON_RETRYABLE` today \u2014 silently defeating this fix's intent for\nthe one scenario where PyGithub's own transport-level retry-until-reset genuinely failed to\nrecover. This is in scope: **add `requests.exceptions.RetryError` to\n`RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`** (`sync_state.py:49-55`), with a comment citing this trace. It is\na one-line, additive change to a tuple that already exists for exactly this class of\ntransport-transient `OSError` subclass, checked in the same branch order, with no new exception\ntype or consumer surface \u2014 sized the same as the rest of this \"Small\" item.\n\n**How \u00a79 (B) changes which path is actually taken**: before \u00a79, every primary-limit 403 on a REST\ncall reached the classifier via *either* this exhausted-budget `RetryError` path (no `retry_after`)\n*or*, if it happened not to exhaust the budget, via a `GithubException`/`RateLimitExceededException`\nraised only after PyGithub had already slept through most of the real wait. \u00a79's bounded retry\nmakes the primary case raise `RateLimitExceededException` **immediately**, with headers intact, on\nthe *first* attempt \u2014 so in practice this `RetryError` fallback becomes the rarer path (reachable\nonly when the bounded-retry subclass's own numeric check does not fire, or when `total` is\nexhausted by an unrelated burst of genuine 5xx errors) rather than the primary route for rate-limit\npropagation. Both this fix (\u00a73.3/\u00a74.1) and \u00a79 stay in the design because each closes a different\ngap: this one is the correctness backstop for classification; \u00a79 is what usually keeps the\n`RateLimitExceededException` path (with a real `retry_after`) live instead of falling through to\nthis backstop.\n\n### 3.4 What was originally out of scope, and is now \u00a79\n\n**Original reasoning (kept for record)**: PyGithub's own synchronous sleep-inside-a-single-call\nbehavior (\u00a73.2, up to ~65 minutes blocking one REST call before any exception or success is visible\nto the caller) is upstream library behavior, not a `sync_state`/`gh_client` classification defect,\nand was not named anywhere in this item's Files, Acceptance Criteria, or Non-Goals. Changing it\nmeans passing a different `retry=` policy to `make_github_client`, which changes behavior for\n*every* PyGithub call in the process, not just the rate-limit classification path \u2014 a materially\nlarger and differently-scoped change that also intersects #3968's \"stop GitHub requests hanging\"\nintent. Recommended filing a separate backlog item referencing this trace as the evidence base.\n\n**Superseded 2026-09-28**: the owner added this to scope in the same instruction as Q6 and the\n`backlog_view` defect. \u00a79 is that design \u2014 scoped narrowly (one subclass, one constructor argument)\nspecifically so it does *not* become the \"materially larger\" change this paragraph originally\nworried about: it changes only the primary-limit branch's backoff, leaving 5xx and secondary-limit\nretry counts and backoff values numerically unchanged (proof in \u00a79).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Component Design\n\n### 4.1 `sync_state.py` (primary edit site)\n\n```python\nfrom github import GithubException, RateLimitExceededException  # add RateLimitExceededException\n\n# New module constants (near existing _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER, line ~334):\n_PRIMARY_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGE_PREFIX: Final = \"api rate limit exceeded\"\n\"\"\"Casefolded prefix of GitHub's primary-quota refusal message. Mirrors PyGithub's\nRequester.isPrimaryRateLimitError (.venv/.../github/Requester.py:971-976), duplicated here for\nthe same reason _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER already is: sync_state must not import gh_client.\"\"\"\n\n_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_HEADER: Final = \"x-ratelimit-reset\"\n_RATE_LIMIT_REMAINING_HEADER: Final = \"x-ratelimit-remaining\"\n_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS: Final = 1.0\n\"\"\"Matches PyGithub's own GithubRetry margin (github/GithubRetry.py: \"plus 1s as it is not clear\nwhen in that second the reset occurs\").\"\"\"\n_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS: Final = 3900.0\n\"\"\"Ceiling for a reset-derived delay (~65 min). GitHub's primary window is 60 min; see\narchitect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a72 Q1 for the margin rationale.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc: GithubException, message: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether a 403 GithubException carries GitHub's primary-quota-exhausted signal.\n\n    Args:\n        exc: The 403 GithubException being classified.\n        message: exc's message text, already casefolded by the caller.\n\n    Returns:\n        True when PyGithub typed exc as RateLimitExceededException, the message starts with\n        GitHub's documented primary-limit wording, or the response carries an exhausted\n        x-ratelimit-remaining header (the fallback for a response body GithubRetry could not\n        parse as JSON \u2014 see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a73.2).\n    \"\"\"\n    ...  # no function body per architecture-boundary convention\n\n\ndef rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(headers: Mapping[str, object] | None) -> float | None:\n    \"\"\"Convert an x-ratelimit-reset header into a bounded, non-negative delay from now.\n\n    Shared by this module's own retry_after_seconds and gh_client._graphql_request's\n    GitHubRateLimitedError construction (Acceptance Criterion 9) -- the one place this\n    epoch-seconds-to-delay conversion lives.\n\n    Args:\n        headers: Response headers, case-insensitively matched like parse_retry_after_header.\n\n    Returns:\n        (reset_epoch - now) + _RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS, clamped to\n        [0.0, _MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS], or None when the header is absent,\n        non-numeric, or the computed delay would be <= 0 (already past). Never fabricates a\n        fallback value -- callers already have their own (see retry_after_seconds's docstring\n        and sync_engine._attempt_sync's max() with the fixed backoff schedule).\n    \"\"\"\n    ...\n```\n\n**`_classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch** (only the `looks_retryable` line changes):\n\n```python\nif status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN:\n    message = _github_exception_message_text(exc).casefold()\n    has_retry_after = parse_retry_after_header(exc.headers) is not None\n    looks_retryable = (\n        has_retry_after\n        or _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER in message\n        or _is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)\n    )\n    return SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE if looks_retryable else SyncErrorKind.NON_RETRYABLE\n```\n\n**`retry_after_seconds`'s GithubException branch** (adds the reset fallback after the existing\n`Retry-After` check, inside the same loop iteration \u2014 no change to the function's loop structure,\ncause-chain walk, or the `GitHubRateLimitedError` branch above it):\n\n```python\nif isinstance(current, GithubException) and current.status in {_HTTP_FORBIDDEN, _HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS}:\n    hint = parse_retry_after_header(current.headers)\n    if hint is not None:\n        return hint\n    reset_hint = rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(current.headers)\n    if reset_hint is not None:\n        return reset_hint\n```\n\n**`RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`** (line 49-55) gains one entry, per \u00a73.3:\n\n```python\nimport requests.exceptions\n\nRETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS = (\n    asyncio.TimeoutError,\n    requests.exceptions.ConnectionError,\n    requests.exceptions.Timeout,\n    requests.exceptions.ChunkedEncodingError,\n    requests.exceptions.ContentDecodingError,\n    requests.exceptions.RetryError,  # new \u2014 see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a73.3:\n                                       # PyGithub's own GithubRetry transport surfaces an\n                                       # exhausted-retry-budget rate limit this way, not as a\n                                       # GithubException.\n)\n```\n\n**`__all__`** (`backlog_core/sync_state.py:57-69`) gains `\"rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds\"` \u2014 confirmed **absent** from the current list, so this is a new export, not an already-present one. Per \u00a78, no new export is needed for `classify_github_failure` \u2014 that name **is** already in `__all__` (same list, confirmed present) alongside `SyncErrorKind`.\n\n**Docstring**: `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring (lines ~507-543) must be\nupdated in the same change to add the primary-limit-signal bullet and the `RetryError` bullet \u2014\nper the item's own Files section, this is the only design record of these rules.\n\n### 4.2 `gh_client.py` (import + two call sites: \u00a74.2a original, \u00a78 new)\n\nImport line (line 76) gains two new symbols \u2014 one already planned (`rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`,\nfor the GraphQL delay fallback below) and one added by \u00a78 (`classify_github_failure`, for\n`probe_backend_status`):\n\n```python\nfrom .sync_state import (\n    RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS,\n    SyncErrorKind,\n    classify_github_failure,     # new \u2014 \u00a78 (A): probe_backend_status reuse\n    classify_sync_error,\n    parse_retry_after_header,\n    rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds,  # new \u2014 this section\n)\n```\n\n**4.2a \u2014 `_graphql_request`'s rate-limited branch** (lines 965-967):\n\n```python\nexcept GithubException as exc:\n    if _is_rate_limited(exc):\n        msg = f\"GitHub GraphQL rate limit exceeded: {_github_exception_message(exc)}\"\n        retry_after = parse_retry_after_header(exc.headers)\n        if retry_after is None:\n            retry_after = rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(exc.headers)\n        raise GitHubRateLimitedError(msg, retry_after=retry_after) from exc\n```\n\nNo other line in `gh_client.py` changes in this sub-section. `_is_rate_limited` (already correct per\nthe item's Impact Radius) is untouched.\n\n**4.2b \u2014 `probe_backend_status`**: see \u00a78 \u2014 this is the owner-added Q6 design, not part of the\nitem's original Files list.\n\n### 4.3 Consumers \u2014 verify only, no interface change\n\n- `sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync`/`_sync_loop` \u2014 consumes `classify_sync_error` and\n  `retry_after_seconds` by their existing signatures; behavior changes only because the two\n  functions now return different values for the same inputs. No code change.\n- `server.py::_retryable`/`_failure_verdict` \u2014 same: consumes `classify_github_failure` and\n  `retry_after_seconds` unchanged.\n- `backends/github_backend.py` (six call sites) \u2014 same: consumes `classify_github_failure`/\n  `classify_sync_error` unchanged.\n- `gh_client.py`'s issue-status-map builder (~line 2291) \u2014 same.\n\n### 4.4 Documentation drift this document does not itself correct (T8)\n\n`backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"Module: github_client.py\" section (~671-675) currently states:\n\"That timeout bounds each provider request, not the whole logical command... every constituent\nprovider request remains bounded to no more than 30 seconds.\" \u00a73.1 and \u00a79.1 show this claim is\n**stale**: `Github(timeout=...)` (`DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 30`) bounds only the gap between two socket\nreads, not a request's total duration, and \u2014 before \u00a79 (B) \u2014 nothing bounded a REST call's\nin-library rate-limit sleep at all; a single primary-limit 403 could block a caller for up to ~65\nminutes inside one call, not 30 seconds. After \u00a79 (B), that in-library sleep is itself bounded (to\neffectively zero \u2014 \u00a79.3), but the doc's specific claim (\"bounded to no more than 30 seconds\") was\nnever true of the wait GithubRetry could impose, before or after this fix. This document does not\nedit `ARCHITECTURE.md` itself \u2014 plan task T8 owns that correction, using this section and \u00a79.1 as\nits source evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Type System Notes\n\nNo new domain identifiers, enums, or Pydantic models anywhere in this document, including the\nthree owner-added items. `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` and `_is_primary_rate_limit_signal` are\npure functions over already-typed inputs (`Mapping[str, object] | None`, `GithubException`, `str`),\nmatching the existing style of `parse_retry_after_header`/`retry_after_seconds` in the same module.\n`Final`-typed module constants for the three new numeric/string literals avoid magic values,\nmatching the file's existing `_HTTP_FORBIDDEN`-style constants. \u00a79's `_BoundedGithubRetry` is a\nthin `GithubRetry` subclass with one overridden method and one new `Final` numeric constant \u2014 no\nnew public type. \u00a710's fix is a single added constructor keyword argument plus one private\nmodule-level coercion function \u2014 no new type anywhere.\n\nBoundary note: `headers` dicts arriving from PyGithub are `Mapping[str, object] | None` with\ncase-normalized-but-untrusted string values (confirmed: PyGithub lowercases header names but\ndoes not validate their values) \u2014 `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` must tolerate a missing key,\na non-numeric string, and a numeric string representing a timestamp in the past, returning `None`\nfor all three rather than raising, matching `parse_retry_after_header`'s existing tolerance\npattern (`ValueError` caught, not propagated).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Testing Architecture\n\nNo new test infrastructure \u2014 extends the existing suites the item's own Impact Radius already\nnamed, plus three more for the owner-added items, using the existing\n`GithubException`/`RateLimitExceededException` direct-construction pattern (not live HTTP fixtures\n\u2014 see \u00a73 for why: `_classify_github_exception` is unit-tested by constructing the exception\ndirectly, independent of whether PyGithub's own transport would have reached it that way in\nproduction).\n\n| Test file | New/changed cases |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/test_retryable_classification.py` | Add a `RateLimitExceededException`(403, no `Retry-After`, primary-limit message) case asserting `retryable: true` at the MCP-response boundary (AC1, AC4). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py` | Add cases for `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`: future reset (positive bounded delay), reset equal to now (per plan decision D1: `(0) + 1.0 = 1.0`, a positive delay, **not** `None`), reset one second or more in the past (per D1: the computed value is `<= 0`, so `None`), missing header (`None`), non-numeric header (`None`), and a corrupted far-future value (clamped to `_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS`, not passed through raw) (AC2). |\n| `tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` | No new cases; run unmodified to confirm the new primary-limit branch is additive (AC6). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` | No new cases; `test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` runs unmodified (AC5). |\n| `tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` | No new cases; runs unmodified (AC7). |\n| `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py` | Add one case: a primary-limit `RateLimitExceededException` wrapped in `ContentUnavailableError` classifies RETRYABLE via the existing cause-chain walk (no new logic, just coverage). Add one case for `requests.exceptions.RetryError` classifying RETRYABLE via the new `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` entry (\u00a73.3, not named in the item's original Acceptance Criteria but required for the fix to be observably effective \u2014 flag this addition explicitly in the task's completion report so review can confirm it against \u00a73.3's evidence). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py` | Add a case for `GitHubRateLimitedError.retry_after` sourced from `x-ratelimit-reset` when no `Retry-After` is present (AC9). |\n| New integration-style case (per AC3, location per implementer's judgment \u2014 likely `test_startup_sync.py` or a `sync_engine`-focused suite) | A primary-limit 403 raised inside `_attempt_sync` retries with a delay rather than setting `SyncStatus.OFFLINE`. |\n| `tests/test_backend_status.py` (owner-added, \u00a78/A, extended 2026-09-28) | Add: a 403 with `RateLimitExceededException`/primary wording \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (regression-proof of the new reuse). Add: a plain `GithubException(403, \"Forbidden\", {})` (no rate-limit signal) \u2192 **no longer** `RATE_LIMITED` (this is the defect AC for A) \u2014 assert `BackendAvailability.ERROR` instead. Add: a 403 with the secondary-limit marker \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (regression check, matches existing classifier behavior). Add: a 429 (any body/message) \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (new \u2014 was `REACHABLE` before this fix, per \u00a78.2's addendum). Add: a 404 and a 500 \u2192 `ERROR` on both (new \u2014 was `REACHABLE` before this fix, per \u00a78.2's \"never `REACHABLE` for a failed count fetch\" rule). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py` (owner-added, \u00a79/B) | Add: `_BoundedGithubRetry.increment()` given a primary-limit 403 response (JSON body, no `Retry-After`, `X-RateLimit-Reset` far in the future) raises `RateLimitExceededException` immediately, with `exc.headers` containing the original `x-ratelimit-reset`/`x-ratelimit-remaining` values. Add: given a secondary-limit 403 response, `increment()` returns a `Retry` whose `get_backoff_time()` is unchanged from `GithubRetry`'s own (60.0s default) \u2014 proves secondary is untouched. Add: given a 500 response, `increment()`'s returned backoff is unchanged from `GithubRetry`'s own default exponential value \u2014 proves 5xx is untouched. Add: given a primary-limit response where the reset is only a few seconds away (computed backoff below the threshold), `increment()` returns a `Retry` object (normal retry-and-wait), not an immediate raise \u2014 proves the threshold, not \"primary vs secondary\", is what gates the behavior. |\n| `tests/test_backlog_core_parsing.py` (owner-added, \u00a710/C) | Add: `view_result_from_local_item(item)` with `item.issue = \"#3978\"` produces `result.number == 3978`. Add: `item.issue = \"\"` (no issue yet) produces `result.number is None`. Add: a malformed `item.issue` (non-numeric after stripping `#`) produces `result.number is None`, not a raised exception. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Non-Goals\n\n- `gh_client._graphql_request`'s rate-limit *detection* (`_is_rate_limited`) \u2014 already correct,\n  not touched, only its delay construction gains the fallback (\u00a74.2a).\n- The secondary-rate-limit branch \u2014 untouched, still additive-only per Q3/AC6, and numerically\n  proven untouched by \u00a79's bounded-retry threshold (\u00a79.2).\n- ~~`gh_client.probe_backend_status` \u2014 out of scope per Q6~~ **superseded 2026-09-28, now \u00a78.**\n- ~~PyGithub's own in-transport retry/backoff configuration (`retry=` argument to\n  `make_github_client`) \u2014 out of scope per \u00a73.4~~ **superseded 2026-09-28, now \u00a79.**\n- No schema, persisted-state, or MCP tool-signature change; `SyncStatus`, `SyncErrorKind`,\n  `BackendUnavailableError`, `GitHubRateLimitedError` all keep their current shape.\n- ~~\u00a710 (C)'s fix touches only `ViewItemResult.number`'s population; leaving dead\n  `ViewItemResultCompact` alone is a Non-Goal~~ **superseded by planner auto-mode decision D4 (SAM\n  plan `P999000ed` context), now \u00a711.** D4 put the deletion of `ViewItemResultCompact` in scope\n  (plan task T7), gated on a search proving it unreferenced \u2014 this is a later, plan-level\n  supersession of this document's own original position, distinct from the repo owner's\n  2026-09-28 scope amendment (which named only Q6, \u00a73.4, and the `number: null` defect).\n- \u00a710 does not touch `server.py`'s `_summary=True` compact-manifest path (the default\n  `backlog_view` shape), which already reports a correct issue number under the key\n  `issue_number` \u2014 derived independently by regex-matching the selector string\n  (`server.py:2085,2101`), not from `ViewItemResult.number`. That path was never broken; \u00a710's fix\n  is for the `summary=False` and MCP-over-budget-compact shapes, which are (\u00a710.2).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Component Design: `probe_backend_status` reuses the classifier (A)\n\n### 8.1 Root cause (confirmed by direct read)\n\n`gh_client.py:2008` defines `probe_backend_status(repo: str = \"\") -> BackendStatus`. Its issue-count\nprobe (`gh_client.py:2043-2049`, confirmed exact line numbers by reading the file):\n\n```python\ntry:\n    open_count: int | None = repo_obj.open_issues_count\n    total_count: int | None = repo_obj.get_issues(state=\"all\").totalCount\nexcept GithubException as exc:\n    if exc.status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN:\n        return BackendStatus(availability=BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED, error=str(exc))\n    return BackendStatus(availability=BackendAvailability.REACHABLE, error=str(exc))\n```\n\n`_HTTP_FORBIDDEN = 403` (`gh_client.py:105`). Every 403 \u2014 regardless of cause \u2014 is reported\n`RATE_LIMITED`. A genuine permission-denied 403 (missing scope, forbidden resource \u2014 the same\ncondition AC5/AC7 in \u00a72 keep `NON_RETRYABLE` in the classifier) is misreported as a transient rate\nlimit here, the opposite error from the one `_classify_github_exception` had (\u00a72's Gap: \"two\ndifferent definitions of a rate-limit 403 coexist\"). A 429 \u2014 GitHub's other rate-limit status code,\nused for both primary and secondary limits \u2014 instead falls to the final `return BackendStatus(\navailability=BackendAvailability.REACHABLE, error=str(exc))` line, since only `status == 403` is\nspecial-cased: a rate limit reported on 429 is misreported `REACHABLE`, the same class of defect as\nthe plain-403 case but in the opposite direction. Every other status (401, 404, 5xx) also falls to\nthat same `REACHABLE` line \u2014 an outright failed issue-count fetch is never reported as anything but\n`RATE_LIMITED` or `REACHABLE` today, with no `ERROR` outcome at all from this `except` block.\n\n### 8.2 Fix: reuse `classify_github_failure`, not a fourth independent signal\n\nThe item names the requirement precisely: \"use the same primary/secondary rate-limit detection the\nclassifier uses.\" The classifier's detection, as of \u00a74.1, is `_classify_github_exception`'s 403\nbranch (`has_retry_after or secondary_marker or _is_primary_rate_limit_signal`) reached through the\npublic, already-`__all__`-exported `classify_github_failure(exc: BaseException) -> SyncErrorKind`\n(`sync_state.py:463-475`), which is exactly \"classify a direct...GitHub failure using the canonical\nstatus rules\" \u2014 it re-dispatches to `_classify_github_exception` for any `GithubException` it is\ngiven. `gh_client.py` already imports from `sync_state.py` in this direction (`RETRYABLE_\nTRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS, SyncErrorKind, classify_sync_error, parse_retry_after_header` at line 76);\nadding `classify_github_failure` to that same import (\u00a74.2) is not a new dependency, only a new\nname from an already-imported module \u2014 the one-way `gh_client \u2192 sync_state` direction is preserved\nexactly, and no fourth independent signal (a re-derived message check local to `gh_client.py`) is\nintroduced.\n\n**2026-09-28 addendum (orchestrator auto-mode, now folded into plan task T3)**: the fix below is\nbroader than \"reuse the classifier for 403.\" The full rule for this `except` block is:\n`RATE_LIMITED` when the status is `429` (unconditionally \u2014 GitHub uses 429 for *both* primary and\nsecondary limits, so there is no \"plain 429\" analogous to a permission-denied plain 403; no\nclassifier call is needed to disambiguate it), **or** when the status is `403` **and**\n`classify_github_failure(exc)` returns `RETRYABLE`; `ERROR` for every other status and verdict,\nincluding 401, 404, and 5xx; **never** `REACHABLE` for a failed issue-count fetch \u2014 an exception\nhere means the fetch did not succeed, and `REACHABLE` claiming otherwise is itself the defect this\naddendum removes, not a quirk to preserve.\n\n```python\n_HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS = 429  # new -- see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a78.2\n\n\ntry:\n    open_count: int | None = repo_obj.open_issues_count\n    total_count: int | None = repo_obj.get_issues(state=\"all\").totalCount\nexcept GithubException as exc:\n    if exc.status == _HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS or (\n        exc.status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN and classify_github_failure(exc) == SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE\n    ):\n        return BackendStatus(availability=BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED, error=str(exc))\n    return BackendStatus(availability=BackendAvailability.ERROR, error=str(exc))\n```\n\n**Why `BackendAvailability.ERROR` for every non-rate-limited status, not `REACHABLE`**:\n`BackendStatus`'s existing early branch for a fully-unreachable repo already uses `ERROR` for\n\"token set but GitHub returned an error (authentication failure, rate limit, or server error)\"\n(`gh_client.py`, the `repo_obj is None` branch) \u2014 `ERROR` is this function's existing vocabulary for\n\"a real, non-transient problem occurred,\" which every exception reaching this `except` block is, by\ndefinition (the issue-count fetch failed). The prior design's `REACHABLE`-with-an-error-string\nfallback for 401/404/5xx was not a deliberate quirk worth preserving; it read as one only because\nthis fix's original scope (A, reusing the classifier) touched exclusively the 403 branch. The\n2026-09-28 addendum extends the same reasoning to the whole `except` block: a repo that raised any\n`GithubException` while being probed did not demonstrate it was reachable.\n\n**Why this is correct given `classify_github_failure`'s broader RETRYABLE cases**: `classify_github_\nfailure(exc)` is only called when `exc.status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN` is already true (short-circuit\n`and`) \u2014 so the only way it returns `RETRYABLE` for this specific `exc` is via the 403 branch's\nthree signals (\u00a72 Q3); a 5xx-triggered `RETRYABLE` verdict from `classify_github_failure` is not\nreachable through this call site because `exc.status` is pinned to 403 by construction at the point\nthe classifier is invoked. The 429 case is handled by its own explicit, unconditional branch instead\nof a classifier call, for the reason given above. No behavior from `classify_github_failure`'s other\nbranches (5xx, `RetryError`-adjacent `OSError` handling in `classify_sync_error` \u2014 a different,\nwider function not used here) leaks into this call site.\n\n### 8.3 Non-regression\n\n- A 403 with `Retry-After` present \u2192 `has_retry_after=True` \u2192 `RETRYABLE` \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (was\n  already `RATE_LIMITED` before this fix; behavior preserved, now for the *correct* reason instead\n  of \"any 403\").\n- A 403 with primary-limit wording / `RateLimitExceededException` / exhausted `x-ratelimit-\n  remaining` \u2192 `RETRYABLE` \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (was already `RATE_LIMITED`; preserved).\n- A 403 with secondary-limit wording \u2192 `RETRYABLE` \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (preserved).\n- A plain `GithubException(403, \"Forbidden\", {})` \u2192 `NON_RETRYABLE` \u2192 **`ERROR`** (was\n  `RATE_LIMITED` before this fix \u2014 this is the defect A exists to correct, not a regression).\n- A 429 (either primary or secondary wording, or no distinguishing message at all) \u2192 the new\n  unconditional branch fires \u2192 `RATE_LIMITED` (was `REACHABLE` before this fix \u2014 a second defect\n  this addendum corrects, not a regression).\n- A 401, 404, or 5xx `GithubException` \u2192 neither the 429 branch nor the `403 and RETRYABLE` branch\n  fires \u2192 `ERROR` (was `REACHABLE`-with-an-error-string before this fix \u2014 corrected by the\n  2026-09-28 addendum's \"never `REACHABLE` for a failed count fetch\" rule, not a regression).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Component Design: bounded in-library rate-limit wait for REST calls (B)\n\n### 9.1 Confirmed absence of any existing bound (\u00a73.1)\n\nNo caller-imposed deadline reaches a REST call made through PyGithub's object interface (\u00a73.1's\n`git show 6befa35e3` evidence: the only deadline mechanism #3968 added, `graphql_total_timeout_\nseconds`/`_call_with_deadline`, is GraphQL-only). `make_github_client`'s implicit `default_retry =\nGithubRetry()` (\u00a73.1) can sleep up to `(x-ratelimit-reset - now) + 1` seconds \u2014 up to ~an hour \u2014\nsynchronously, inside a single REST call, before that call returns to its caller in any form\n(success, exception, or otherwise). The owner's default decision governs: **no GitHub call may\nblock hour-scale on a rate limit; bound the in-library wait, and surface a longer wait promptly as\na retryable failure carrying `retry_after`** (which \u00a74.1's classifier fix then correctly marks\n`RETRYABLE` with an accurate `retry_after`, sourced from `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`).\n\n**Documentation note (see \u00a74.4)**: `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s `github_client.py` section\n(~671-675) makes the same \"bounded to no more than 30 seconds\" claim this section's evidence\nalready refutes. That claim is stale and plan task T8 corrects it \u2014 this document only names the\ndrift; it does not edit `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n\n### 9.2 Design: `_BoundedGithubRetry`, a numeric-threshold override of `increment()`\n\nAdd to `github_client.py` (which already imports `Requester` and constructs the `Github` client;\ngains `GithubRetry` from the `github` package and `json` from the stdlib):\n\n```python\nimport json\n# ... existing imports ...\nfrom github import Auth, Github, GithubRetry\nfrom github.Requester import HTTPRequestsConnectionClass, HTTPSRequestsConnectionClass, Requester\n\n_MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS: Final = 150.0\n\"\"\"Ceiling for a single in-transport rate-limit sleep GithubRetry may perform before this client\nraises instead of waiting. Deliberately set above the two backoff paths this must NOT touch: the\nsecondary-limit branch's `max(secondary_rate_wait, retry.get_backoff_time())` -- NOT a flat\nsecondary_rate_wait value (GithubRetry.py:163-174 computes backoff = secondary_rate_wait, then\ntakes the max with retry.get_backoff_time()) -- which is itself bounded by urllib3's default\nbackoff_max (120.0s, urllib3.util.retry.Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX), so this branch's backoff never\nexceeds max(60.0, 120.0) = 120.0 under this client's unmodified secondary_rate_wait default (60.0s,\nGithubRetry.DEFAULT_SECONDARY_RATE_WAIT); and the plain 5xx retry path, whose backoff is also\nbounded by that same 120.0s backoff_max. 150.0 is therefore a numeric no-op for both and fires only\nfor a primary-limit backoff computed from x-ratelimit-reset, which is the only case capable of\nexceeding it under this client's unmodified defaults. If either default is ever overridden higher,\nraise this constant to match -- see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a79.2 and \u00a79.4.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass _BoundedGithubRetry(GithubRetry):\n    \"\"\"GithubRetry that raises instead of sleeping through an hour-scale primary-limit backoff.\n\n    GithubRetry.increment() (github/GithubRetry.py) already classifies a 403 correctly as\n    primary-limit, secondary-limit, or a genuine non-rate-limit refusal, and for the two rate-limit\n    cases returns a Retry object whose get_backoff_time() is monkey-patched to the wait it computed\n    -- resetBackoff + 1 for primary (potentially ~3600s), max(secondary_rate_wait,\n    retry.get_backoff_time()) for secondary (<=120s under this client's unmodified defaults, see\n    \u00a79.4 -- NOT a flat secondary_rate_wait value). This subclass inspects only that returned\n    numeric value: when it exceeds\n    _MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS (only reachable for the primary case under this client's\n    defaults -- see that constant's docstring), it raises the same GithubException PyGithub would\n    eventually raise on total exhaustion (via Requester.createException, so it is correctly typed\n    RateLimitExceededException with the original response headers intact -- see\n    architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a73.2/\u00a79.2), immediately instead of after sleeping.\n    Every other returned value, and every exception GithubRetry.increment() itself raises (the\n    non-rate-limit 403 path, and MaxRetryError once genuinely exhausted), passes through unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def increment(\n        self,\n        method: str | None = None,\n        url: str | None = None,\n        response: Any = None,\n        error: Exception | None = None,\n        _pool: Any = None,\n        _stacktrace: Any = None,\n    ) -> Retry:\n        retry = super().increment(method, url, response, error, _pool, _stacktrace)\n        if response is not None and retry.get_backoff_time() > _MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS:\n            content = json.loads(self.get_content(response, url))\n            raise Requester.createException(response.status, response.headers, content)\n        return retry\n```\n\n**Open verification point (unresolved by this document): does the second `self.get_content(response,\nurl)` call see a consumed stream?** `GithubRetry.increment()` (the `super().increment()` call above)\nalready called `self.get_content(response, url)` once, on the same `response`/`HTTPResponse` object,\nto inspect the rate-limit message (`GithubRetry.py:120`, \u00a73.2). `get_content` is a `staticmethod`\nthat wraps the raw `HTTPResponse` in a fresh `requests.Response` and reads `.content` \u2014 it does not\ncache across calls, so whether this override's *second* call returns the same bytes depends on\nwhether the underlying `HTTPResponse` itself caches its body after a first read (version- and\nconfiguration-dependent behavior this document has not verified against the installed `urllib3`).\nIf the second read instead returns an empty or truncated body, `json.loads(...)` raises, and the\n`except (MaxRetryError, GithubException): raise` framing this override sits outside of does not\napply \u2014 an uncaught `JSONDecodeError` would propagate in place of the intended\n`RateLimitExceededException`, losing both the correct exception type and the original body. **T4\nmust add a test asserting the raised exception's body/content is intact** (spec \u00a79.2's own\n`increment()`-raises-immediately test case, extended to assert on `content`/`exc.data`, not only on\n`exc.headers`). **Named fallback if the second read is not reliable**: capture `content` once, from\nthe same `response` object, before calling `super().increment(...)` (i.e. read the body in this\noverride prior to delegating, and pass the captured bytes to `Requester.createException` instead of\nre-invoking `self.get_content`), rather than relying on a second read of a possibly-consumed stream.\n\nWired into `make_github_client` (line 912) via one new constructor argument, no other change:\n\n```python\nreturn Github(\n    auth=Auth.Token(resolve_token(token)),\n    base_url=resolved_base_url,\n    timeout=timeout,\n    retry=_BoundedGithubRetry(),\n)\n```\n\n`_BoundedGithubRetry()` with no arguments keeps every other `GithubRetry`/`Retry` default\n(`total=10`, `status_forcelist` = 403 + 500-599, `secondary_rate_wait=60.0`,\n`allowed_methods` = `GET`/`POST` plus urllib3's defaults) \u2014 this change touches exactly one\nnumeric threshold on exactly one branch's return value, nothing else.\n\n### 9.3 Why this satisfies \"bound the wait, surface promptly, carry `retry_after`\"\n\n- **Bound**: when the primary-limit backoff exceeds 150s, `increment()` raises *before* any sleep\n  occurs \u2014 `super().increment(...)` only computes the backoff and monkey-patches\n  `get_backoff_time`; the actual `time.sleep()` call is made by urllib3's own `Retry.sleep()`\n  *after* `increment()` returns, which this override intercepts by raising first. The bound on the\n  in-library wait for the primary case is therefore **zero additional sleep**, not merely \"smaller\n  than an hour.\"\n- **Surface promptly**: the raised exception is `Requester.createException(response.status,\n  response.headers, content)` \u2014 by construction (\u00a73.2, confirmed by direct read of\n  `Requester.py:936-963`) this returns `RateLimitExceededException` whenever `isRateLimitError`\n  is true, which it is here (that is exactly why `GithubRetry.increment()`'s own logic computed a\n  rate-limit backoff for this response in the first place). It propagates out of the single REST\n  call immediately.\n- **Carries `retry_after`**: because the exception is a correctly-typed `GithubException` with\n  `response.headers` intact (not a headers-less `RetryError`), \u00a74.1's `retry_after_seconds`\n  reaches its `GithubException` branch, finds no `Retry-After` (this is the no-`Retry-After` case\n  by construction \u2014 GithubRetry's own logic only computes this backoff when `Retry-After` is\n  absent, \u00a73.2), and falls through to `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(current.headers)`, producing\n  a real, bounded (\u00a72 Q1, \u22643900s) `retry_after` from the same `x-ratelimit-reset` header PyGithub\n  itself would have used. This is materially better than the pre-B alternative where the same\n  condition, if it happened to exhaust `total` instead, produced a `RetryError` with no headers and\n  therefore no `retry_after` (\u00a73.3) \u2014 B makes the accurate-`retry_after` path the common case for\n  REST primary-limit refusals instead of the `RetryError` fallback.\n\n### 9.4 Preservation proof for secondary-limit and 5xx retry behavior\n\nThe override's `if` only fires when `retry.get_backoff_time() > 150.0`. The two cases that must\nstay untouched are numerically incapable of reaching that value under this client's defaults:\n\n- **Secondary limit**: `GithubRetry.increment()` initializes `backoff = self.secondary_rate_wait`\n  for the secondary branch (`GithubRetry.py:163`), but that is not the branch's final value \u2014 lines\n  165-174 then compute `retry_backoff = retry.get_backoff_time()` and take `backoff = retry_backoff`\n  whenever `retry_backoff > backoff`, for both the primary and secondary branches alike. The\n  secondary branch's actual backoff is therefore `max(secondary_rate_wait, retry.get_backoff_time())`,\n  **not** a flat `secondary_rate_wait` \u2014 confirmed by direct read of `GithubRetry.py:163-174`.\n  `_BoundedGithubRetry()` does not override `secondary_rate_wait` (stays\n  `DEFAULT_SECONDARY_RATE_WAIT = 60`, `GithubRetry.py:51`) or `retry.get_backoff_time()`'s own\n  ceiling, which is urllib3's `backoff_max` (`Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX = 120`,\n  `urllib3/util/retry.py:210`, unmodified). So the secondary branch's backoff is bounded by\n  `max(60.0, 120.0) = 120.0` under this client's unmodified defaults \u2014 still `120.0 < 150.0`, so\n  the override's `if` never fires for this branch; `retry` is returned unchanged, byte-for-byte the\n  same object `GithubRetry.increment()` itself produced. (An earlier revision of this document\n  stated the secondary bound as a flat 60s; that was wrong about the mechanism, not the conclusion \u2014\n  120.0 is still below the 150.0 threshold, so the preservation claim itself holds.)\n- **5xx**: a non-403 response never enters `GithubRetry.increment()`'s custom branch at all (its\n  body is gated by `if response.status == 403:`); it falls straight to `super().increment(...)`\n  (`GithubRetry.py:208`, i.e. `urllib3.util.retry.Retry.increment`), whose own backoff is bounded by\n  `backoff_max` (`Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX = 120`, confirmed `urllib3/util/retry.py:210`,\n  unmodified by `GithubRetry.__init__` or by `_BoundedGithubRetry`). `120.0 < 150.0` \u2014 the\n  override's `if` never fires for 5xx either, on any attempt number, because urllib3's own backoff\n  computation is provably capped below the threshold regardless of how many attempts have already\n  occurred.\n- Both bounds (60 and 120) are library defaults this design does not modify; the 150.0s threshold\n  constant's own docstring (\u00a79.2) documents this coupling explicitly so a future change to either\n  default is caught by re-reading that comment rather than silently breaking the proof above.\n\n### 9.5 Non-regression for the \"not a rate limit\" and \"already exhausted\" paths\n\nBoth remaining branches of `GithubRetry.increment()` raise directly, before ever returning a\n`Retry` object for this override to inspect \u2014 `_BoundedGithubRetry.increment()`'s body only runs\nits `if` after `super().increment(...)` has already returned normally, so:\n\n- A plain 403 (`Requester.createException` called by `GithubRetry.increment()` itself when\n  `isRateLimitError` is `False`, \u00a73.2) raises straight through `super().increment(...)`; this\n  override's `if` is never reached; behavior is identical to today.\n- A malformed-JSON-body 403 (the plain `GithubException` at `GithubRetry.py:126-129`, \u00a73.2's third\n  bullet) likewise raises inside `super().increment(...)`; identical to today. (This is the case\n  Q3's header-only fallback signal exists for once it *does* reach the classifier, \u00a72 Q3 item 3 \u2014\n  unrelated to this override, which never sees it.)\n- Once `total` is genuinely exhausted, `super().increment(...)` itself raises `MaxRetryError`\n  (\u00a73.3) before returning anything this override could inspect; propagates unchanged into the\n  `RetryError` path \u00a73.3/\u00a74.1 already handles.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Component Design: `backlog_view`/`backlog view` `\"number\": null` (C)\n\nThis defect is unrelated to A/B/the rate-limit fix in every other section of this document. It was\nadded to this item's scope by the owner in the same instruction as Q6 and \u00a73.4 (\"add them to scope.\nlooks aligned to me.\"), observed live on #3978 itself.\n\n### 10.1 Reproduction\n\n```bash\nuv run plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/cli.py backlog view --selector \"#3978\"\n```\n\nRan against this checkout. The returned JSON's top-level keys (parsed and enumerated, not visually\nskimmed) are: `added, body, body_truncated, description, errors, file_path, groomed, issue, labels,\nmessages, milestone, plan, priority, section_filter_miss, sections, sections_index,\nsections_metadata, source, state, status, status_source, title, unavailable_capabilities,\nwarnings`. **`number` is absent from the CLI's JSON entirely** \u2014 not present as `null`, simply not\na key \u2014 while `issue` (a string, `\"#3978\"`) is present and correct. This differs from the item's\nliteral description (\"returns `\\\"number\\\": null`\"); \u00a710.2 traces why both observations are true\nsymptoms of the same root cause, on different response shapes.\n\n### 10.2 Root cause: a Pydantic field with no producer\n\n`ViewItemResult.number: int | None = None` (`models.py:1812`) is declared, defaulting to `None`,\nand is **never assigned by any code path that constructs a `ViewItemResult`** for the view command.\nConfirmed by reading every constructor site:\n\n- `parsing.view_result_from_local_item(item)` (`parsing.py:1425-1444`), the **sole** producer of a\n  populated `ViewItemResult` for both backend branches \u2014 `operations._resolve_view_source` calls\n  `result = view_result_from_local_item(item) if item else ViewItemResult()` (`operations.py:4270`)\n  *before* its `if getattr(backend, \"supports_github_extras\", False):` branch, so this same\n  constructor call is what the GitHub backend (this repo's configured backend, `.dh/config.yaml`\n  `backend.name: github`) uses too \u2014 sets `title=item.title, priority=item.section,\n  issue=item.issue, plan=item.plan, file_path=item.file_path, groomed=item.metadata.groomed`, then\n  separately assigns `result.description`, `result.source`, `result.added`, `result.body`,\n  `result.status` \u2014 **`number` is never among them.**\n- No other function in `operations.py`, `server.py`, or `parsing.py` assigns `.number` on a\n  `ViewItemResult` instance anywhere (confirmed: no `\\.number\\s*=` match against a `ViewItemResult`\n  instance in `backlog_core/*.py` outside this producer's absence).\n\n`item.issue` (`BacklogItem.issue: str = \"\"`, `models.py:1001`) already holds the correctly-resolved\nvalue \u2014 for #3978, `\"#3978\"` \u2014 it is simply stored under the `issue` field (a `str`), never coerced\ninto the separate `number` (`int | None`) field the model also declares. This is a straightforward\nmissing-assignment defect, not a resolution or coercion-logic bug: the raw value is already present\nand correct on `item`, and the pattern to convert it already exists elsewhere in this same package\n(`operations.py:4220`, `_live_lookup_id`: `item.issue.lstrip(\"#\") or None`).\n\n**Reconciling the CLI-vs-MCP wording difference (\u00a710.1)**: `sam_schema/cli.py`'s `backlog view`\ncommand (`sam_schema/backlog.py:139-172`) calls `operations.view_item(...)` and passes the raw\n`ViewItemResult` straight to `cli_output.output_json(result)`, whose docstring (`cli_output.py:33-\n58`) states it calls `model_dump_json(by_alias=True, exclude_none=...)` with `exclude_none=True` by\ndefault \u2014 so a `None`-valued `number` field is **dropped from the key set entirely**, matching\n\u00a710.1's observed output exactly. The MCP `backlog_view` tool (`server.py:2290`+), by contrast,\nbuilds its own plain `dict[str, object]` by hand for at least one response shape \u2014 the over-budget\ncompact response (`server.py:2213`, confirmed by direct read): `\"number\": result.number` is written\nunconditionally, so that same always-`None` value serializes as the literal `\"number\": null` the\nitem's text describes. Both are downstream symptoms of the identical root cause (\u00a710.2's missing\nassignment); they differ only in which response-serialization boundary each interface happens to\nuse. (The MCP tool's default `summary=True` shape is unaffected either way \u2014 see \u00a77's note: it\nderives a working `issue_number` independently, by regex-matching the selector text, not from\n`result.number` at all.)\n\n### 10.3 Fix\n\nOne added keyword argument at the single producer site (`parsing.py:1425-1432`), plus one small\nprivate coercion helper in the same file \u2014 `parsing.py` is documented as \"pure functions with no\nGitHub or typer dependencies\" (`parsing.py`'s module docstring), and `artifact_migration.py`\n(home of the closest existing analogue, `_migrate_coerce_issue`) itself imports *from* `parsing.py`\n(`from .parsing import parse_backlog`), so reusing that helper directly would invert the existing\nimport direction \u2014 this fix does not add that dependency; it duplicates the same small, already-\nproven-safe coercion locally, matching the precedent \u00a74.1 already established for\n`_is_primary_rate_limit_signal`/`_PRIMARY_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGE_PREFIX` (small, tolerant duplication\nacross an import boundary, in preference to a new cross-layer dependency):\n\n```python\ndef _coerce_issue_number(issue: str) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"Parse a BacklogItem.issue string (\"#3978\", \"3978\", or \"\") into its numeric issue number.\n\n    Args:\n        issue: The item's issue field, as stored on BacklogItem -- \"#\"-prefixed, bare, or empty.\n\n    Returns:\n        The positive integer issue number, or None when issue is empty or not numeric. Never\n        raises -- mirrors parse_retry_after_header's tolerance pattern for an untrusted string.\n    \"\"\"\n    stripped = issue.lstrip(\"#\").strip()\n    if not stripped:\n        return None\n    try:\n        return int(stripped)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\n\ndef view_result_from_local_item(item: BacklogItem) -> ViewItemResult:\n    \"\"\"Build view result from a local backlog item.\n\n    Returns:\n        ViewItemResult with title, priority, issue, number, plan, file_path, groomed, and\n        optionally description/source/added/status from the per-item file.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = ViewItemResult(\n        title=item.title,\n        priority=item.section,\n        issue=item.issue,\n        number=_coerce_issue_number(item.issue),  # new\n        plan=item.plan,\n        file_path=item.file_path,\n        groomed=item.metadata.groomed,\n    )\n    # ... unchanged from here ...\n```\n\nThis is the only edit. It fixes `number` for every consumer that reads `ViewItemResult.number`\n(the MCP over-budget compact dict at `server.py:2213`, and any future consumer of the field) in one\nplace, without touching `_resolve_view_source`'s two-branch structure, the CLI's `exclude_none`\nserialization policy, or the already-correct `summary=True` `issue_number` derivation (\u00a77).\n\n### 10.4 Non-regression\n\n- `item.issue = \"#3978\"` \u2192 `number = 3978`. Matches the reproduction target exactly.\n- `item.issue = \"\"` (a backlog item with no linked GitHub issue yet, e.g. a fresh native-backend\n  item pre-sync) \u2192 `number = None`. Same externally-visible result as today (`None`), now for a\n  documented, tested reason instead of an unpopulated field.\n- A malformed `item.issue` (should not occur given how `issue` is written elsewhere in this\n  package, but the boundary must not raise) \u2192 `number = None`, not an exception \u2014 matches\n  `_coerce_issue_number`'s explicit `try`/`except ValueError` and the tolerance precedent already\n  established by `parse_retry_after_header` (\u00a75's Boundary note) and `_migrate_coerce_issue`.\n\n\n---\n\n## 11. Component Design: delete dead `ViewItemResultCompact` (D4)\n\nThis item is unrelated to A/B/C and to the rate-limit fix in every other section of this document.\nIt is not part of the repo owner's 2026-09-28 scope amendment (Executive Summary) \u2014 that amendment\nnamed only Q6, \u00a73.4, and the `backlog_view` `number: null` defect (\u00a710). It is a separate, later\naddition from the SAM plan `P999000ed` context's own planning decisions: \"D4 (orchestrator): delete\ndead `ViewItemResultCompact` only after a search proves no reference (T7). This supersedes spec\n\u00a77's Non-Goal on that class.\"\n\n### 11.1 Evidence\n\n`ViewItemResultCompact` (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:1854`) declares the\nsame dead `number: int | None = None` field \u00a710 fixes on `ViewItemResult`, but \u2014 confirmed by\n`grep -rn \"ViewItemResultCompact(\" backlog_core/*.py` returning no constructor call anywhere in the\npackage \u2014 the class is never instantiated. It is unused dead code, not a second instance of \u00a710's\ndefect: there is no producer to fix because there is no live consumer to fix it for.\n\n### 11.2 Gate and scope (D4, task T7)\n\nDeletion is conditional, not unconditional: it proceeds **only after** a search proves zero\nhand-authored references outside the class's own definition. Task T7 owns both the search and the\ndeletion:\n\n1. `git grep -n \"ViewItemResultCompact\"` and `grep -rn \"ViewItemResultCompact\" --exclude-dir=.venv`\n   \u2014 record the raw output and classify each hit (definition, hand-authored reference, or\n   generated-doc mention).\n2. Delete the class from `models.py` only when the only hits are the definition itself.\n3. After deletion, `git grep -n \"ViewItemResultCompact\" -- ':!plugins/development-harness/\n   graphify-out'` must return nothing.\n\n**`graphify-out/` mentions do not block deletion.** That directory holds generated documentation\nregenerated from source, not hand-authored references to the class \u2014 a mention there is evidence of\nwhat the generator produced from a prior state, not a live dependency on the class continuing to\nexist. T7's own gate (step 3 above) explicitly excludes that path from the post-deletion check\nrather than treating any mention inside it as a reason to keep the class. This is the same\nreasoning \u00a710 already applied when confirming `ViewItemResultCompact` had zero constructor calls \u2014\nD4 extends that same evidence to a deletion decision instead of a Non-Goal.\n\n### 11.3 Non-regression\n\n- `ViewItemResult` (the class \u00a710 actually fixes) is untouched \u2014 only `ViewItemResultCompact` is\n  removed.\n- No test in `tests/test_backlog_core_server.py`, `tests/test_backlog_core_parsing.py`,\n  `tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`, or `backlog_core/tests/test_view_over_budget_gate.py`\n  constructs or imports `ViewItemResultCompact` (consistent with \u00a711.1's grep evidence); all must\n  continue to pass unmodified after the deletion."}